WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.965 --> 00:00:03.978
[torch hissing]

00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:06.729
[saw scraping]

00:00:06.729 --> 00:00:09.562
[file scratching]

00:00:10.578 --> 00:00:11.700
My name is Macy,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.283
and I am a jeweler at Maejean Vintage.

00:00:16.505 --> 00:00:18.192
I am going to walk you through

00:00:18.192 --> 00:00:21.830
how to restore a 1930s art deco ring.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.980
This ring is a 14-karat
yellow gold setting.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.660
The gemstone in this ring
is a created spinel stone.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.480
The biggest things wrong with this ring

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.840
were the misshapen band,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.550
there's a resizing bar

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.340
at the bottom of the ring band
from the previous resizing,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.810
and then the gemstone
will need to be replaced,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.270
as it's not in any salvageable condition.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:50.060
This ring might probably
only be worth maybe $100,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.730
and when we're finished,

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:56.260
this ring might sell for
about $4,000 to $5,000.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.270
The first thing that I will
do with any restoration

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.960
is to take out any gemstones
that can't be saved.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.790
There's a large part on
the underside of this stone

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.240
that had cracked off.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.656
I am getting rid of the resizing band

00:01:15.656 --> 00:01:17.322
because oftentimes,

00:01:17.322 --> 00:01:20.620
especially in vintage and antique pieces,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.960
the resizing was often poorly done.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.010
This tool is called a jeweler's saw,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.460
and I'm using this to
cut out the sizing bar

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.080
rather than unsoldering it,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.901
just because I was having
some trouble unsoldering it.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.470
So just to be safe and
eliminate the chance

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.360
of accidentally melting the whole ring

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.509
while trying to unsolder it,

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:40.992
I just decided to cut it out.

00:01:40.992 --> 00:01:42.440
Soldering is a process

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.020
of joining two pieces
of metal with solder,

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:50.850
and that solder can be either
silver solder or gold solder.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.560
When I remove that piece of metal,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.580
solder will only flow
if it's a perfect seam.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.560
So once I cut the sizing bar out,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.090
I have to file the ends

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.640
so they're perfectly
parallel to each other

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.650
so that when I close that seam
there's no air in between,

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.073
so that the solder can flow.

00:02:12.526 --> 00:02:14.080
[file scratching]

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.820
I am hammering the seam shut,

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.300
so I'm trying to close
those two ends together.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.770
Even if the ring band
isn't perfectly round,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.330
that can always be fixed later,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.110
as long as those two ends
are touching each other

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.420
to get them ready to be
soldered back together.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.970
And then I take the ring
to my soldering bench,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.895
and I'm going to prep
the ring for soldering.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.420
I apply what is called flux,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.500
and flux will prevent any oxides

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.500
that will form on the surface of the ring

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.740
while being soldered from
the torch from building up,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.010
and then adding my gold solder to the seam

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.730
where I intend to have it flow.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.358
Then I'm going to be using
a 14-karat-gold solder,

00:02:59.358 --> 00:03:03.720
which is essentially just an
alloy of gold and also zinc,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.080
and zinc is what makes the
metal melt at a low temperature.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.400
So it allows the solder
to flow into the seam

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.160
of what I'm trying to connect,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.140
and it will bind those two
pieces together permanently.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.400
And then I use my torch to
evenly heat up the piece

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.343
in order to get the solder up
to its melting temperature.

00:03:25.325 --> 00:03:28.580
[torch hissing]

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.200
The prongs on this ring are
cracked and worn from age.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.310
They just need a little
bit of reinforcement.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.370
I add some solder to the prongs

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.653
to give them some extra thickness

00:03:38.653 --> 00:03:40.161
so I can create a new seat

00:03:40.161 --> 00:03:42.260
later on in the process for the stone.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.848
[torch hissing]

00:03:49.848 --> 00:03:51.030
[flex shaft buzzing]

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.490
Once I'm finished with the prongs,

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.830
I take the ring back to my bench

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.650
and remove some of the
extra solder material

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.470
from the inside of the ring band.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.637
I use a tool called a flex shaft

00:04:02.637 --> 00:04:04.990
with a silicone bit,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.290
and that's going to remove

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.631
some of the material from the solder seam

00:04:08.631 --> 00:04:10.740
so that when I hammer it round again,

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:15.280
the solder doesn't embed
itself into the metal.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.120
Once that material is mostly removed,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.250
I move on to hammering

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.220
a round shape back into the ring

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.750
with a rawhide mallet
and a steel ring mandrel.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.660
And since the steel
ring mandrel is tapered

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.970
to accommodate for all different
kinds of sizes of rings,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.810
I frequently flip back and forth

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.870
and work from both sides to make sure

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:42.160
that one side is not
larger than the other side,

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.490
and I work from all sides of the piece

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.240
except for on the ring head,

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.023
because I don't want to
deform the head of the ring.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.510
Once I'm done rounding the band back out,

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:04.190
I go ahead and I start
removing some of the solder

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.070
from the outside of the band,

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.870
and I use this with the hand file.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.960
And then once I'm finished
with the hand file,

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.880
I start using some
silicone polishing wheels

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.100
with my Foredom flex shaft

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.270
to further remove some of the scratches

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.365
and begin the process of
starting to polish the piece.

00:05:22.365 --> 00:05:25.918
We go through a process of
four different abrasivenesses,

00:05:25.918 --> 00:05:28.840
starting from the most
coarse to the finest,

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.080
to get the bulk of the polishing done

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.538
on the outside of the piece.

00:05:33.538 --> 00:05:36.330
Once I'm done working on the ring band,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.840
I move on to polishing
the inside of the ring,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.667
starting with a polishing compound

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:44.530
that's called White Diamond.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.820
And this is an abrasive
polishing compound,

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.090
so even though it's polishing,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.760
it still removes material
from the surface of the metal.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:52.710
And then, after that,

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.530
I move on to a polishing
compound called Fabulustre,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.955
which is a polishing compound

00:05:57.955 --> 00:06:00.820
that is very tiny clay bits,

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.760
so it kind of brings out that really

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.790
luster, high-polish finish to the metal.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.690
And once I'm done with that,

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.360
I repeat the entire process
on the outside of the ring

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.345
so that I can get the ring
back to a mirror finish,

00:06:16.345 --> 00:06:18.431
like it should be.

00:06:18.431 --> 00:06:22.464
[polishing tool buzzing]

00:06:22.464 --> 00:06:24.950
Once I'm done with polishing,

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:29.278
I move on to getting the
stone ready to be set.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.180
I'm replacing the created
spinel with a diamond,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.576
because it's a large enough setting

00:06:36.576 --> 00:06:39.382
that it would make a really
nice engagement ring.

00:06:39.382 --> 00:06:41.590
It's a solid ring setting.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.235
This diamond is 1.3 carats.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.690
To prepare for the diamond to be set,

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.220
I kind of evaluate where
all the prongs are at,

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.370
I make sure that they're all even,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.550
they're all nice and reinforced

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.410
from the soldering that
I have already done,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.960
and I start to carve out the material

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.350
with some setting burrs and ball burrs

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:07.560
to try and create a more perfect seat

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.650
for the diamond to sit in.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:10.700
Once that's done,

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.722
I use a little bit of wax

00:07:12.722 --> 00:07:16.383
to place the stone into the ring setting.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.430
And from there, since it's a good fit,

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:21.690
I'm ready to start
pushing the prongs over,

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.950
and so I use a tool that
is called a bezel pusher

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.600
to start to push over the prongs

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.017
to hold the diamond in place.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.560
We are so happy with
how this ring came out.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.518
We're so happy to give it a second life.

